{
  "id": "Compound::DB01200 - Gene::3358",
  "verbalization": "Bromocriptine, a member of the ergoline class, is linked to Htr2c through a series of molecular and functional relationships. The evidence suggests that Bromocriptine, as part of the ergoline family, binds directly to Htr1d, which in turn participates in serotonin binding. This chain of interactions implies that Bromocriptine may influence Htr2c through a serotonergic pathway, as Htr1d's role in serotonin binding could mediate its effect on Htr2c. The shared ancestry of Bromocriptine with other ergolines, such as Cabergoline, further supports a common mechanism of action, with both compounds binding to Htr2c. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Bromocriptine in modulating serotonin signaling, possibly through Htr1d, which may serve as a bridge between ergoline compounds and Htr2c. The evidence underscores a complex, multi-tiered relationship where Bromocriptine's effects on Htr2c are mediated through a cascade involving serotonin binding and receptor participation.",
